{"path": "index.html", "content": "<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Sky – Bilingual Explanation</title>
<style>
  body {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif; margin:0; padding:0; color:#333;
    background:#f0f8ff; /* Day mode default */
    transition: background-color 0.5s ease, color 0.5s ease;
  }
  body.night-mode {
    background: #0a0a2e; /* Night mode dark sky */
    color: #e0e0e0;
  }
  /* Simple starry background for night mode */
  body.night-mode::before {
    content: "";
    position: fixed;
    top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%;
    background: 
      radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #fff 1px, #0a0a2e 1px),
      radial-gradient(circle at 80% 80%, #fff 1px, #0a0a2e 1px);
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 0;
  }
  .container { position: relative; z-index: 1; display:flex; height:100vh; }
  .panel { flex:1; padding:2rem; overflow:auto; }
  .panel h1 { font-size:1.8rem; margin-top:0; color:#2c3e50; }
  .panel h2 { font-size:1.4rem; margin-top:1.5rem; color:#2980b9; }
  pre { background:#ecf0f1; padding:1rem; border-radius:4px; overflow:auto; }
  .laTeX { font-family: "Times New Roman", serif; font-style:italic; }
  .toolbar { position:absolute; top:1rem; right:1rem; display:flex; gap:.5rem; }
  footer { text-align:center; padding:1rem; background:#eaeaea; margin-top:2rem; font-size:0.9rem; color:#555; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="toolbar">
  <button id="theme-toggle" aria-label="Toggle theme">🌙</button>
</div>

<div class="container">
  <!-- English panel -->
  <div class="panel" id="english">
    <h1>Why Is the Sky Blue?</h1>

    <h2>For a 3‑Year‑Old Toddler</h2>
    <p>Sunlight looks white, but it's actually made of all the colors of the rainbow. When sunlight hits the air around Earth, the blue part of the light bounces around in all directions—kind of like when you spray water from a bottle and it spreads out mist. That's why when you look up, your eyes see blue everywhere! The other colors mostly pass right through.</p>

    <h2>For a High School Teenager</h2>
    <p>Sunlight contains all visible wavelengths, but Earth's atmosphere is full of gas molecules like nitrogen and oxygen. Blue light has a shorter wavelength and scatters off these molecules much more strongly than red light does—a process called <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>. Because blue scatters in every direction, the whole sky glows blue when you look up. At sunset, sunlight travels through much more atmosphere, and most of the blue has already scattered away, leaving the reds and oranges to reach your eyes.</p>

    <h2>For a Physics PhD</h2>
    <p>The sky's color is described by <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong> in the regime where particle size \(a \ll \lambda\) (molecular nitrogen/oxygen, \(\sim 0.3\,\text{nm}\), versus visible \(\lambda \sim 400\text{--}700\,\text{nm}\)). The differential scattering cross‑section for unpolarized light is</p>
    <p class="laTeX">\[
    \frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega} = \frac{k^4 \alpha^2}{2} (1 + \cos^2\theta),
    \]</p>
    <p>with \(k = 2\pi/\lambda\) and polarizability \(\alpha \propto r^3\). The total cross‑section scales as \(\sigma \propto \lambda^{-4}\), meaning shorter (blue) wavelengths scatter roughly \( (700/450)^4 \approx 6\times \) more intensely than red. At sea level, molecular number density \(n \sim 2.5\times10^{25}\,\text{m}^{-3}\) sets the observed radiance \(I(\theta) \propto n \lambda^{-4} (1 + \cos^2\theta)\). The \(\sim 90^\circ\) enhancement relative to the sun–earth line, altitude‑dependent thinning of \(n\), and the transition to Mie scattering by aerosols at longer path lengths (producing red sunsets) are all consistent with this framework. Polarization properties (\(I_{\parallel} \neq I_{\perp}\)) and the \(\lambda^{-4}\) law serve as a classic undergraduate/graduate optics benchmark.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- Chinese panel -->
  <div class="panel" id="chinese">
    <h1>天空为什么是蓝色的？</h1>

    <h2>给3岁的幼儿</h2>
    <p>阳光看起来是白色的，但实际上由彩虹中所有的颜色组成。当阳光击地球周围的空气时，蓝色的光以各个方向散开——这有点像喷水瓶喷出的雾。这就是为什么向上看时，你的眼睛到处都看到蓝色！其他颜色大多穿透过去。</p>

    <h2>对一名高中生</h2>
    <p>阳光包含所有可见波长，但地球的大气中充满了氮气和氧气等气体分子。蓝光的波长较短，比红光散射到这些分子上强得多——这是一种<strong>瑞利散射</strong>过程。因为蓝光在各个方向散射，整个天空在你仰望时都发蓝。日落时，阳光必须穿过更多的大气，绝大多数蓝光已经散开，留下红橙色到达你的眼睛。</p>

    <h2>对一位物理博士</h2>
    <p>天空的颜色由<strong>瑞利散射</strong>描述，在粒子尺寸 \(a \ll \lambda\) 的情境下（分子氮/氧，\(\sim 0.3\,\text{nm}\)，与可见光 \(\lambda \sim 400\text{--}700\,\text{nm}\) 相比）。对于非偏振光的散射截面差分为</p>
    <p class="laTeX">\[
    \frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega} = \frac{k^4 \alpha^2}{2} (1 + \cos^2\theta),
    \]</p>
    <p>其中 \(k = 2\pi/\lambda\) 且极化率 \(\alpha \propto r^3\)。总截面按 \(\sigma \propto \lambda^{-4}\) 缩放，这意味着较短（蓝色）波长比红光散射强约 \( (700/450)^4 \approx 6\) 倍。在海平面上，分子数密度 \(n \sim 2.5\times10^{25}\,\text{m}^{-3}\) 确定了观测到的辐射度 \(I(\theta) \propto n \lambda^{-4} (1 + \cos^2\theta)\)。相对于日地线的 \(\sim 90^\circ\) 增强、随高度变薄的 \(n\) 以及在较长路径长度下由气溶胶产生的米散射（产生红日落）与该框架一致。极化属性 (\(I_{\parallel} \neq I_{\perp}\)) 以及 \(\lambda^{-4}\) 法则是经典的本科/研究生光学基准。</p>
  </div>
</div>

<footer>
  © 2026 Jennifer Robins
</footer>

<script>
  document.getElementById('theme-toggle').addEventListener('click', function() {
    document.body.classList.toggle('night-mode');
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>"}

